"""just settings: each setting's value and where it was set, for one role, one variable or all of them; --value
sets one in local.yml, and --reset puts one back to its default.

    "$bin/python" tools/settings --usage [--json=true]
    "$bin/python" tools/settings [--vault-password-file <file>] [--all=true] [--changed=true] [--json=true] -- [NAME]
    "$bin/python" tools/settings [--vault-password-file <file>] --value=<value> [--json=true] -- NAME
    "$bin/python" tools/settings [--vault-password-file <file>] --reset=true [--json=true] -- NAME

It asks Ansible itself, in-process, as a run would: the loader with the vault opened as Ansible's own commands open
it, the inventory, the variables of a play that names the role - on a host of the group that role's playbook runs it
on - and the templar for each value. Nothing of Ansible's precedence is written again here. What a setting is - each
role's argument_specs, the wiring, the sections of local.yml.example - comes from tools/docs.py, the same model the
wiki's reference pages are generated from (docs/architecture/custom-code.md §7.9).

Reading changes nothing and prints no secret:

- Lookups are held to an allowlist where Ansible loads every lookup, however one is reached: pipe runs a command,
  password writes a file. The inventory is read by the yaml plugin alone, never script, which runs what it finds.
- A value is masked when the vault file holds it (lock 1), when Ansible marks it as decrypted from a vault, an inline
  !vault included (lock 2), when its role marks the variable no_log (lock 3), or when its expression reads a name
  the vault holds or a no_log variable (lock 4). Before anything is printed, every value about to be printed is
  checked against every secret Ansible decrypted; a match prints nothing and exits 1 (lock 5).
- The vault's password file is the one the recipe passes (.vault_pass, where there is one), else what
  ansible-playbook would take; a vault that cannot be opened is refused before anything is printed.

--value writes one setting into local.yml and --reset takes one out, nothing else anywhere (Settings.write and
Settings.reset):

- Never a secret: anything the vault holds, feeds or a role marks no_log, and a value vaulted inline in local.yml,
  is refused for just vault, the value unsaid. Nor what is not one setting for every VM: a name made from each VM's
  own, what a run sets for itself, the Proxmox host's address (hosts.yml).
- A role variable fed by a setting of local.yml.example is written as that setting (login_desktop for
  claude_code_login_desktop). The value is held to its type as the role's argument_specs give it, by Ansible's own
  validator, and is a value, never an expression: {{ }}, {% %} and {# #} are refused.
- As just init writes: JSON on one line, in place of the line that set it (and its value's further lines), else of
  its example's commented line, else at the end. The file is read back before it replaces the old one, atomically,
  with its mode: every other key as it was, an inline !vault included, or nothing is written.
- --reset takes the setting's line out - a role variable's own too, where one is set by hand beside the setting
  that feeds it - and puts its example's commented line back, as just init found it. Taking a line out can make no
  value wrong, so it is refused only a secret and the host's address, as --value is; the same read back holds it.

Exits 0 with the report or the write, 1 when it refuses.
"""

